## REASONING

**Decomposition.** The request is one primary goal — resolve the basin's elevation bands with a justified column count — but "resolve elevation" in a snow-dominated Cascade basin like the Upper Yakima implicitly carries a validation goal (SNOTEL peak-SWE) and a partitioning-understanding goal (how snow vs. rain and soil control ET/runoff/recharge across the gradient).

**Feasibility of the elevation driver.** This is the crux. The Upper Yakima has ~1900 m of relief and sits inside CONUS, so the NLDAS-2 default forcing (0.125°, ~12 km) DOES vary precipitation and temperature orographically across the basin — a relief this large should span many distinct NLDAS cells, giving a genuine (if 12-km-smoothed) elevation-climate signal. So elevation acting through the seasonal snowpack and the rain-snow transition IS partially answerable, unlike the uniform-forcing case. The honest limit: sub-12 km terrain is unresolved, so ridgetop SWE will be underestimated and narrow valley/aspect effects are lost. Elevation is therefore a legitimate stratification axis here, but I pair every elevation band with soil so I don't misattribute soil-driven partitioning to elevation.

**What the stack cannot do.** USGS gauges are listed as available observations, but the single-column stack has no routing/aggregation — there is no native integrated streamflow to compare to a gauge. I record streamflow validation as not-answerable and put routing in the backlog. SNOTEL peak-SWE IS in default output and IS comparable (co-located), with the caveat of ridgetop underestimation.

**Snow/recharge target ⇒ spin-up.** Snowpack is a fast state, but if any target touches soil moisture recharge or water table, cold-start artifacts dominate. I specify a WTD-informed warm start (Fan 2013) plus a few spin-up years.

**N.** Elevation is the headline driver: I use 5 bands (~500–800, 800–1200, 1200–1600, 1600–2000, 2000–2400 m) chosen to bracket the rain-snow transition (~1000–1600 m). Crossed with 2 dominant SSURGO soil groups = 10 exploratory columns, with light replication (2) at the rain-snow transition band where behavior is most sensitive → 12 exploratory. Validation columns are pinned to actual SNOTEL sites in-basin (count unknown until the SNOTEL locator runs).

